From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Henrik Klee <henrik@goat-code.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 10:41:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] bls: Find the boot partition on an LwVM disk

iOS partitions the flash of the 32-bit devices with LwVM, not GPT. On the
32-bit build the boot manager falls back to that table: partitions whose
chunks are stored in order are listed as one range each, with their type
and identity in network byte order as LwVM keeps them. The boot partition
is the XBOOTLDR one, as on a GPT disk; a root may be the Discoverable
Partitions ARM root or plain Linux filesystem data, which is how Orchard's
installer marks its LwVM partition.

A 32-bit entry's kernel is a zImage: it is checked by its magic and loaded
with a zero word behind it, so that a kernel built to accept an appended
device tree does not take what follows for one.

On the 64-bit build only the GPT is read, as before.

Assisted-by: LLM
